3. FUNDAMENTAL MODEL


---

3.1 JD AS A GRAPH

JD is represented as:

G(JD) = (Nodes, Edges, Axes)

Where:

Nodes = JD entries

Edges = JNL / dependency / system relationships

Axes = time, system, tag, compression state



---

4. QUERY ARCHITECTURE


---

4.1 QUERY LAYERS

All queries pass through 5 layers:

1. Parse Intent
2. Resolve JNL / Tags / Anchors
3. Select Subgraph (LAL routing)
4. Temporal Filter (JIP-0608-D)
5. Reconstruction Engine (Yggdrasil state rebuild)


---

5. QUERY LANGUAGE (JQL — JD QUERY LANGUAGE)


---

5.1 FORMAT

SELECT <ENTITY>
FROM JD
WHERE <CONDITIONS>
AT <TIME>
MODE <TRAVERSE | RECONSTRUCT | COMPRESS | TRACE>


---

5.2 EXAMPLES

Example 1 — System Query

SELECT ODIN
FROM JD
WHERE system = "ODIN"
MODE TRACE


---

Example 2 — Temporal Query

SELECT JD
FROM JD
WHERE date = 0608
MODE RECONSTRUCT


---

Example 3 — Full System Reconstruction

SELECT YGGDRASIL
FROM JD
AT 2026-06-08
MODE RECONSTRUCT


---

Example 4 — Tag-Based Traversal

SELECT *
FROM JD
WHERE tags CONTAINS #routing
MODE TRAVERSE


---

6. TRAVERSAL MODES


---

6.1 TRAVERSE MODE

Purpose:

explore relationships

walk graph structure


Behavior:

no reconstruction

no temporal rollback

lightweight navigation



---

6.2 RECONSTRUCT MODE

Purpose:

rebuild system state at timestamp


Behavior:

applies JIP-0608-D backfill rules

resolves CHAOS snapshots

reconstructs Yggdrasil snapshot



---

6.3 TRACE MODE

Purpose:

follow lineage of a single entity


Behavior:

backward + forward dependency mapping

JNL chain resolution

mirror tracing (JMS)



---

6.4 COMPRESS MODE

Purpose:

generate GRAVEYARD or JORMUNGANDR representations


Behavior:

reduces JD graph into minimal form

preserves structure but removes verbosity



